## Summary

Simplicity audit of main at 46e759d, surveying shared store transactions, worker delivery, generated tables, HTTP and client routing, app wiring, page handlers, and previous cleanup findings. Selected one structural simplification.

Before: `stageContent` in `internal/db/extract.go` required callers to pair a pending write batch with a separately managed content reuse map keyed by a concatenated hash and MIME type.
Now: `pendingWrites.content` in `internal/db/writes.go` owns reuse tracking keyed directly by the hash/type pair. All 13 call sites use the batch method, including the existing sweep test; wiki, extraction, and photo generation no longer manage companion maps.

This makes the batch own its staging state and prevents callers accidentally supplying a fresh or unrelated reuse map. Visible behavior, content uniqueness, sweep protection, and schema remain unchanged. No migration, production write, compatibility path, or test seam was needed.

Fourteen focused existing tests passed in two timeout-30 runs, covering wiki cards, extraction, photo processing, concurrent reuse, sweep races, PDF page uploads, fetched content reuse, stock images, and thumbnails. Repository-wide go vet, gofmt, and git diff --check passed. Review finished with no findings. The requested merge of main reported already up to date; the worktree was clean, with no conflicts or further edits.

Left generated-cache readiness cleanup alone as lower value and confined to its existing builder; avoided broad rewrites.

## Reviews

### Snapshot #1, reviewer "Review", grok grok-4.7: done

Content rows for one write batch are now reused by the batch itself. `stageContent` used to take a separate map, keyed by hash and MIME type, that each caller had to keep next to a `pendingWrites` so the same bytes became one `CONTENT` insert. That map is now `pendingWrites.contents`, filled by `pendingWrites.content`. Every caller, including the sweep race in `sweep_test.go`, goes through that method. Lookup, the error when a row was swept before its bytes were stored again, and the server-side insert are the same.

No significant findings.
